WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.700
<v ->And next we talk about AWS config.</v>

2
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.560
So it's a service to help you audit

3
00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:08.340
and record compliance of your AWS resources.

4
00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.310
Why? Because you can record the configurations

5
00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:12.870
and their changes to configuration

6
00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:15.300
of your resources over time.

7
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.310
Now, when you define rules to make sure

8
00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:19.470
that these resources configurations

9
00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.630
are compliant or not over time,

10
00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:25.140
these rules are just here to evaluate compliance.

11
00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:27.150
They're not here to prevent actions

12
00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:29.760
from happening on your resources.

13
00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:32.400
So for example, say you have a compliance rule

14
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.640
to look at whether or not SSH is opened

15
00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.200
on any of your security group

16
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.510
because you are not using SSH in your company,

17
00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:42.570
maybe using something like SSM systems manager.

18
00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:45.390
So in this case, if someone creates a change

19
00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:48.750
in your security group rule and allows SSH

20
00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:51.300
the config rule does not prevent this from happening.

21
00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:52.290
There is no deny rule.

22
00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:54.420
This is a very good use case instead

23
00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.130
for something like an SCP.

24
00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:58.890
Instead, what the config rule will be doing

25
00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:01.080
will be to look at the compliance of your security group.

26
00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:02.550
It will give you a notification

27
00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:04.740
to your admins through an SNS topic

28
00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:07.740
that, yes one of your security group has changed

29
00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:09.210
and it's not compliant anymore

30
00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:11.160
and you have to act upon it.

31
00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.170
Now, there are some automations you can build,

32
00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:15.300
but remember, config rules are not here

33
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:16.800
to prevent actions from happening.

34
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.330
They're here to give you the compliance

35
00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:20.280
of your resources over time.

36
00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:23.322
Now, what types of questions can be solved by AWS config?

37
00:01:23.322 --> 00:01:26.224
For example, is there unrestricted SSH access

38
00:01:26.224 --> 00:01:27.811
to my security group?

39
00:01:27.811 --> 00:01:32.280
Or do my buckets have any public access in S3?

40
00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.970
Or how has my ALB configuration changed over time?

41
00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:37.140
Maybe someone did a mistake

42
00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.330
and I want to backtrack that change.

43
00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:41.970
You can receive alerts on SNS topics

44
00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:43.560
for any kind of changes,

45
00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:46.470
and AOS config is a per region service,

46
00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:47.430
so you need to enable it

47
00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:49.650
in every region where you need it to be.

48
00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:52.290
Also, there is a possibility to aggregate all

49
00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:54.960
the AWS config data across accounts and regions

50
00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:56.550
into one central account,

51
00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:59.370
which is a good use case for a security account

52
00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:02.105
where you have login, config and so on.

53
00:02:02.105 --> 00:02:04.620
Now, config resource, you can view the compliance

54
00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:05.490
of a resource over time.

55
00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:07.920
So if some rules apply to that resource

56
00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.620
then you can see it's red when it's not compliant.

57
00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:11.920
And then as soon as you apply a rule

58
00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:14.340
it becomes green and becomes compliant.

59
00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.740
You can view the resource configuration over time

60
00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:18.660
so you can see when the changes happen

61
00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:20.460
and what changes happen.

62
00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:24.090
And finally, in case you have collateral API calls enabled,

63
00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:26.550
you can view who made these changes,

64
00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:29.010
when the changes happen directly from AWS config,

65
00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:30.840
which is very handy.

66
00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.390
So a bit more details on config rules themselves.

67
00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:36.660
So we can first use AWS managed config rules

68
00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:39.090
Over 75 managed rules we can use.

69
00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:40.920
So it gives us a lot to play with,

70
00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:42.420
but we can also if you wanted to,

71
00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:44.940
create our own custom configuration rules

72
00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:46.650
in this way we would need to create

73
00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:50.310
a Lambda function to perform it and link it with config.

74
00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:52.620
So for example, we can create a Lambda function

75
00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:56.370
that checks if EBS disc is of type gp2

76
00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:59.430
or if any two instance is of type t2.micro.

77
00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:00.263
So this could be helpful.

78
00:03:00.263 --> 00:03:02.370
For example, if you have a development environment

79
00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:04.290
and you wanted to limit your cost.

80
00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:05.880
So you would create a Lambda function

81
00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:09.330
and then create the custom config rule associated with it.

82
00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:11.520
Then you can evaluate or trigger the rule

83
00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.920
for every config change in AWS.

84
00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:17.137
For example, any time an EBS disc is created

85
00:03:17.137 --> 00:03:18.810
then do this rule.

86
00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:22.380
Or if you wanted to just at a regular time interval.

87
00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:26.040
So for example, every one day, scan all my EBS discs

88
00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:27.930
and make sure that they are compliant.

89
00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:29.310
It's up to you.

90
00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:31.530
You can also trigger Amazon EventBridge

91
00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:33.524
in case a rule is noncompliant.

92
00:03:33.524 --> 00:03:34.988
So thanks to EventBridge,

93
00:03:34.988 --> 00:03:38.363
you can catch any noncompliance and you can trigger any kind

94
00:03:38.363 --> 00:03:41.910
of destination that is a real destination of EventBridge

95
00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:45.060
which allows you a lot of different integrations.

96
00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.550
But config rules also has a deeper integration

97
00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:51.921
with SSM automations, such as if a rule is not compliant.

98
00:03:51.921 --> 00:03:53.820
If a resource is not compliant,

99
00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:56.070
then you can trigger an auto remediation

100
00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:58.650
by triggering an SSM automation.

101
00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:01.440
So with SSM automation, you can do a bit more

102
00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:03.240
than what you could do with the Lambda function

103
00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:06.720
for example, you can change right away security group rules

104
00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:10.890
or you can stop instances with non-approved tags and so on.

105
00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:13.170
So the actions can be directly written

106
00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:16.290
in an automation document in SSM.

107
00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:19.020
Okay, so that's it for AWS config.

108
00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:22.020
I hope you liked it, and I will see you in the next lecture.

